---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Pixel Art Maker for Kids — Learn Coordinates & Patterns | Kids Code"><div class="wrap"><div class="page-head"><span class="eyebrow">The pixel studio · Create something tiny and mighty</span><h1>Big imagination.<br/>Little squares.</h1><p>Pick a color. Tap a square. Make a creature, a pattern, or something nobody has ever seen. Your canvas is a 12 × 12 grid of possibilities.</p></div><div class="activity-shell"><section class="panel"><div id="pixel-grid" class="pixel-grid" role="group" aria-label="Pixel canvas, 12 columns and 12 rows"></div><p class="tiny" id="pixel-position" aria-live="polite">Tap any square to paint. Use arrow keys to move between pixels.</p><div class="control-row"><button class="button dark" id="pixel-download">Download my art ↓</button><button class="button ghost" id="pixel-undo">Undo</button><button class="button ghost" id="pixel-clear">Clear</button></div><div class="feedback" id="pixel-feedback" role="status">Your art saves in this browser when storage is available.</div></section><section class="panel"><h2>Your creative toolkit</h2><label>Pick your color</label><div class="palette" id="palette"></div><label><input type="checkbox" id="pixel-mirror"/> Mirror left and right</label><p class="tiny">A mirror makes a matching pixel on the opposite side. That’s symmetry!</p><h3 style="margin-top:26px;font-size:1.3rem">Paint with coordinates</h3><p class="tiny">A coordinate is a pixel’s address: column (x), then row (y). We count from 0 at the top-left, just like many programs do.</p><div class="control-row"><label for="pixel-x">x <input id="pixel-x" type="number" min="0" max="11" value="0" style="width:76px"/></label><label for="pixel-y">y <input id="pixel-y" type="number" min="0" max="11" value="0" style="width:76px"/></label><button class="button small" id="pixel-plot">Paint pixel</button></div><details><summary>See your picture as data</summary><p class="tiny">Each number is a palette color. Each line is one row of your picture. Computers can store images as grids of values like these.</p><pre id="pixel-data" style="font-size:.75rem;line-height:1.6"></pre></details><div class="note"><strong>Try a mini mission:</strong> draw a robot with matching eyes, make a checkerboard, or use only three colors. Paint at least 6 squares to collect your Pixel Maker discovery.</div><noscript><p>Turn on JavaScript to use the pixel canvas.</p></noscript></section></div></div><script>import '../../scripts/pixel';</script></Layout>
